<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>com.xander</groupId>
        <artifactId>xander-lab-parent</artifactId>
        <version>0.0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>
        <relativePath>../../pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>xander-webmvc-support</artifactId>
    <name>xander-webmvc-support</name>
    <description>
        Servlet MVC support (V9 §20): the request/response shaping every MVC service needs,
        and nothing else.

        Contents:
        - GlobalExceptionHandler          -- maps every exception to the shared Result envelope.
        - ResultStatusResponseBodyAdvice  -- keeps the HTTP status aligned with Result.code.
        - AuthInterceptor                 -- the MVC auth adapter (V9 §20 calls it that).
        - WebConfig                       -- the WebMvcConfigurer: interceptor + CORS registration.

        V9 §20 audience: "仅 MVC 服务使用". The WebFlux Gateway must NOT depend on this
        module -- it carries spring-webmvc and jakarta.servlet, which is the same accident as
        putting spring-boot-starter-web on a WebFlux classpath.

        Dependency scope, and why spring-boot-starter-web is `provided`:
        The consuming service supplies the MVC runtime. Declaring it `compile` would make this
        module pull Tomcat into anything that depends on it, which is exactly the failure V9
        §19 split the super-common.jar to prevent. `provided` keeps the compile surface honest
        and is still on the test classpath, so this module's own tests run against it.
        spring-boot-starter-data-redis is `provided` for the same reason -- see the note on it
        below.

        Deliberate non-members, and why:
        - PasswordEncoderConfig  -> stays on the application. It wires spring-security-crypto's
          BCrypt bean; V9 §20 does not list it, and it is identity-domain configuration rather
          than MVC request shaping.
        - ShedLockConfiguration  -> stays on the application. V9 §20 does not list it either;
          it is scheduler wiring, not MVC.

        Note on packages: the classes moved here keep no business-domain package. AuthInterceptor
        used to live in com.xander.lab.identity.security; a library must not ship a package in a
        business domain's namespace, because that namespace is a domain in the scanner and the
        library would look like part of that domain. This is the same call xander-auth-core made
        when UserContext / JwtUtil moved from identity.security to auth.core.
    </description>

    <dependencies>
        <!-- Result, Constants. Version managed by the reactor (same ${project.version}). -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.xander</groupId>
            <artifactId>xander-model</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <!-- UserContext (trusted identity) and JwtUtil (token verifier, incl. the role claim). -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.xander</groupId>
            <artifactId>xander-auth-core</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <!--
            AccessTokenLookupPort               resolves a bearer token, so AuthInterceptor no
                                                longer injects identity's AuthService.
            FeatureModelNotConfiguredException  GlobalExceptionHandler maps it to HTTP 400.

            A note on the second one: it makes this module depend on a business contract, which
            no other library does. The alternative was to split GlobalExceptionHandler across two
            advices, which puts Spring's "most specific handler wins across advices" ordering on
            the critical path of every error response. V9 §20 lists GlobalExceptionHandler whole,
            so the whole class moved and the dependency is recorded here rather than left
            implicit. If a future MVC service must not carry core-api, the split is the escape
            hatch, and it is a behaviour-visible change, not a refactor.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.xander</groupId>
            <artifactId>core-api</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <!--
            The MVC runtime: @RestControllerAdvice, ResponseBodyAdvice, WebMvcConfigurer,
            HandlerInterceptor, jakarta.servlet. `provided`; see the description above.
            Version managed by the Spring Boot BOM.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
            <scope>provided</scope>
        </dependency>
        <!--
            GlobalExceptionHandler maps RedisConnectionFailureException / RedisSystemException
            (spring-data-redis) and DataAccessException (spring-tx) to HTTP 503. These are
            *class literals inside @ExceptionHandler*, so the types must be on the runtime
            classpath, not just the compile one.

            `provided` for the same reason as spring-boot-starter-web: every MVC service in this
            repo already declares it (apps/core-host/pom.xml does), and making it `compile` would
            force Redis onto a pure-REST MVC service that has no cache at all. The obligation this
            transfers is a *loud* one: a consumer without it fails at startup while registering
            the advice, not silently at request time. If a MVC service ever must run without
            Redis, the escape hatch is to split this one handler out of GlobalExceptionHandler;
            V9 §20 lists the class whole, so the class moved whole and the split is recorded as a
            behaviour-visible change rather than done quietly here.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-redis</artifactId>
            <scope>provided</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.projectlombok</groupId>
            <artifactId>lombok</artifactId>
            <optional>true</optional>
        </dependency>

        <!--
            The library owns its own tests. AuthInterceptorTest and GlobalExceptionHandlerTest
            moved here with their classes: a library whose tests stay in the application module
            has not really been extracted.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <!-- No spring-boot-maven-plugin: see libs/xander-model/pom.xml for why. -->
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
